<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">

  <PropertyGroup>
    <RootNamespace>Craticula.EntityFrameworkCore</RootNamespace>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <!-- The FIRST NuGet dependency of any project under csharp/src/, and AR-3 is what permits it:
         "compile-only core packages have zero database driver dependencies. Execution adapters are
         separate packages that depend on the ecosystem's drivers." This project is one of those
         adapters (AR-4), so the dependency is the point rather than a compromise.

         Exactly ONE package, and Relational rather than the base one: two of the APIs this adapter
         needs live there and have no non-relational equivalent — EF.Functions.Collate, which is how
         SEM-1's case mechanism is realized, and ToTable for the property-bag model. It therefore
         declares what this adapter is: an adapter for relational providers, which is also all AR-4
         claims. It pulls Microsoft.EntityFrameworkCore in transitively; naming both would pin two
         versions that must move together anyway.

         NO provider package (Npgsql.EntityFrameworkCore.PostgreSQL, .SqlServer, .Sqlite), which is
         load-bearing rather than tidy: a provider reference would make this package usable with one
         database, and its provider-specific extensions (EF.Functions.ILike, EF.Functions.Glob) would
         be in scope and would get used. The engine differences reach this adapter as DATA instead —
         see EfCoreProviderSemantics' five facts. A provider is the host's choice, declared next to
         its DbContext, and the test project references Sqlite for exactly that reason.

         Version centrally managed in csharp/Directory.Packages.props (10.0.11, the release that
         targets net10.0, which every project here does) — this file names only the package. -->
    <PackageReference Include="Microsoft.EntityFrameworkCore.Relational" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <!-- Craticula.Core ONLY. Not Craticula.Sql: this is AR-4's own EF-specific exception (#1646 found
         and corrected the earlier over-generalization here — it is NOT a rule that an execution
         adapter may never depend on a compiler; Craticula.Dapper depends on Craticula.Sql for real,
         a normal, intended production edge per docs/architecture/c4-containers.md's
         `Rel(sqlc, dapper, "{sql, params[]}")`). This adapter specifically compiles IR straight to
         IQueryable<T> expression trees and never produces SQL text at all, so it has no artifact to
         consume — which is why the four rules it shares with the SQL compiler live in Craticula.Core
         rather than being reachable from here (SqlPatternEscaping, FilterNodeFields, IsoInstant,
         SemanticsResolver). Not Craticula.AgGrid either: the IR is the contract, and a second grid
         vendor must be able to drive this adapter. Not Craticula.Export.Csv, though this adapter
         yields ExportRow — that type moved into Craticula.Core.Export precisely so an adapter is not
         welded to one file format. -->
    <ProjectReference Include="../Craticula.Core/Craticula.Core.csproj" />
  </ItemGroup>

</Project>
